  "notes": "WHAT THE NUMBERS MEAN\nWall time includes HTTP and stream completion. TTFT ends at the first content or reasoning delta.\nChunk gaps are not exact token latency: a chunk may hold several tokens.\nTPOT proxy = (last - first generation arrival) / (completion_tokens - 1).\nDecode proxy = its inverse. Prefill proxy = prompt_tokens / TTFT, so it includes queueing,\ntokenizing and the first decode step.\nPhases come from the engine's own /profile, one row per turn. Engine counters come from its log.\nRequests are sequential and shuffled each round. Warmups and failures are left out of statistics.\nEach request has a unique prefix to limit prefix reuse. A few repetitions give limited confidence.\n\nGPU IN-FLIGHT PERCENT\nTime with at least one job between \"handed to the GuC\" and \"fence signalled\", from xe tracepoints.\nIt includes GuC scheduling and time other VFs held the engine, so it is an upper bound on how busy\nthe EUs were, not an EU utilization. It cannot separate XMX from EU work.\n\nMICROBENCHMARKS (vkprobe)\nActive load through Vulkan with the engine's own Mesa, run while the engine is idle. VRAM buffers\nhold random data so Battlemage's compression cannot flatter them. xmx_* rates from registers show\nwhat the matrix units can do; the from-memory rates show what a shader reloading operands gets.\n\nPROFILING COST (decode speed against Light, measured on this machine, 6 requests each)\nLight            /proc, gateway, xpu-smi                 no measurable cost\nStandard         + xe job trace, CPU hotspots            -6%\nDeep             + perf stat on the engine (IPC, misses) -23%\nperf stat is the expensive one: the engine switches context about 9000 times a second, and on a VM\neach switch with PMU counters active traps to the hypervisor. So take speed from Light runs and\nreasons from Standard or Deep runs, and only compare runs of the same level.\n\nHYPERVISOR COUNTERS CHANGE THE ENGINE TOO, THE OTHER WAY\nWith hostagent.py streaming, decode runs about 19% faster (measured, 6 requests each): xpu-smi\npolling the GPU accounts for about 16%, the xe PMU's engine counters for about 11%; host CPU and\nuncore counters had no effect. Both touch the GPU's firmware. The clock barely moves during decode\n(about 1200-1350 MHz either way), so the likely cause is the GPU leaving idle states less often\nbetween decode's short jobs. That mechanism is not confirmed yet. So the live hypervisor stream runs at Standard and Deep only,\nnever at Light, and a run that had it is marked. The hypervisor's configuration snapshot is a\none-off read and is always taken.\n"
